  "question": {
    "text": "The time period of a simple pendulum is&nbsp;<math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math>&nbsp;when its point of suspension is at rest with respect to the ground. When the point of suspension moves vertically upwards according to the law&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>ms</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>, its new time period is <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math>, then the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo></math>Take&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>ms</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></math>",
